\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-2 We showed that learned post-hoc correction of frozen flow-matching VLAs is harmful by default. Under a K-matched design ($K{=}2$, same $\varepsilon$ and CRM checkpoint), unconditional correction degrades all four LIBERO suites on SmolVLA (0.4B, up to $-$14.8\,pp on Long) and all four suites on $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B, up to $-$17.0\,pp on Long; preliminary single-seed). The root cause is that 80--97\% of control steps are already confident; corrections on these steps inject noise. Calibrated confidence sets from $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distances detect confident steps and gate the CRM, recovering nearly all degradation ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}2.8$ to $+$14.5\,pp). This is a diagnostic contribution: calibrated gating makes learned correction modules safe to deploy; without one, VLA-only remains the simpler choice. The key finding is that \emph{knowing when not to correct} matters more than correction strength.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\label{sec:limitations}
\looseness=-2 The pairwise $L_2$ probe measures sample disagreement, not action correctness; confidently wrong steps escape gating. Our finding is established for the tested lightweight MLP-based CRM (327K parameters, $L_2$ loss, offline training); whether larger architectures or online fine-tuning would change this conclusion remains open. On $\pi_{0.5}$, gating recovers only roughly half of the degradation (Appendix, Table~\ref{tab:tau_sweep_pi05}), suggesting effectiveness depends on CRM correction quality. The threshold $\tau$ is suite-dependent (Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep}); all experiments use LIBERO simulation, and real-robot transfer remains future work.

